      "problem": "What is the inverse of $f(x) =\\ln(x+3)+6?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+3)+6\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3) & =x-6 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)} & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y+3 & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y & ={e}^{x-6}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = e^{x-6}-3.$",
